@page "/exsheet/workbook"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Sheet

<DocsPage Product="ExSheet" Title="Workbook"
          Lead="Several Sheets in one ExSheet, as an Excel workbook holds them: a tab for each under the grid, Formulas that read one Sheet from another, and one document that saves them all.">

    <DocsSection Title="A Workbook in one ExSheet">
        <p>
            Bind <code>Workbook</code> in place of <code>Document</code>, and ExSheet shows every Sheet of the
            <code>WorkbookDocument</code> you hand it, one at a time in its one grid, with the Sheet Tabs under the
            grid. After every change it raises <code>WorkbookChanged</code> with a new <code>WorkbookDocument</code>
            — every Sheet in order, the Sheet shown and the Linked Tables' declarations — and stores nothing itself,
            as it does with a Sheet Document.
        </p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>&lt;ExSheet @@bind-Workbook="_workbook" /&gt;</code></pre>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>Set one of the two.</b> <code>Document</code> and <code>Workbook</code> set together throw: there would
                be two answers to which Sheets the component shows. A Sheet Document opens as a Workbook of that one
                Sheet, and <code>WorkbookDocument.FromJson</code> reads one too.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>DocumentChanged</code> is raised while the Workbook holds one Sheet</b>, so a page bound to
                <code>Document</code> works as it always has. Once it holds several, a Sheet Document cannot say what
                it holds: <code>DocumentChanged</code> stops, <code>ToDocument()</code> throws, and
                <code>WorkbookChanged</code> and <code>ToWorkbookDocument()</code> carry the Workbook.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>A Formula reads another Sheet by naming it</b>, <code>=Claim!D7</code> or
                <code>=SUM('Q1 Sales'!B2:B9)</code>, and a change on any Sheet recalculates what reads it on every
                Sheet. <a href="exsheet/formulas#the-engine-on-its-own">The engine on its own</a> says what a rename,
                a deletion and an inserted row do to those References.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>One undo stack covers the Workbook.</b> <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Z</kbd> undoes the latest operation
                whichever Sheet it was made on, and every command from your code is one step on it. As in Excel, an undo
                or a redo shows the Sheet its step was taken on and selects the range it changed — the cell typed in, the
                range pasted, filled or formatted, the whole rows or columns inserted or deleted — with the Focus on its
                top-left cell, so you see what came back. <code>UndoAsync</code> and <code>RedoAsync</code> do the same.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(WorkbookTabs)" Mud="typeof(WorkbookTabsMud)" CodeOpen="true"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Sheet/Expenses.cs"])">
            The claim, and a summary that reads it. Change a quantity on Claim, then select the Summary tab. Type
            <code>=</code> in an empty cell of Summary, press the Claim tab and press a quantity: the Formula reads
            <code>=Claim!</code> and that cell, and <kbd>Enter</kbd> puts it on Summary. "Add a
            sheet" inserts an empty Sheet after the last, named <code>Sheet</code> and the next number no Sheet has,
            the line beside the buttons says what the Sheets are now, and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Z</kbd> takes it out
            again. The tabs do the same themselves: press <code>+</code>, double-click Claim and type a new name, or
            right-click a tab for Delete, Move Left, Move Right and Copy, or drag a tab onto another, and watch
            Summary's Formulas follow. Change a quantity on Claim, select Summary and press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> +
            <kbd>Z</kbd>: Claim is shown again with that cell selected.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Commands name their Sheet">
        <p>
            A command from your code says which Sheet it acts on: <code>DoAsync("Claim", edit)</code> does the edit on
            Claim whether Claim is shown or not, and <code>CellFormatAt("Claim", address)</code> reads a cell there.
            While the Workbook holds one Sheet, <code>DoAsync(edit)</code> and <code>CellFormatAt(address)</code> act on
            it as they always have. Once it holds several they are refused with
            <code>SheetRefusalReason.SheetNotNamed</code> and change nothing: taken against the Sheet shown, what they
            did would depend on whether the user's press on a tab reached the component first.
        </p>
        <pre class="docs-code"><code>await sheet.DoAsync("Claim", SheetEdit.Enter(CellAddress.Parse("B2"), "120"));
await sheet.DoAsync("Summary", SheetEdit.Rename("Totals"));
await sheet.DoAsync("Totals", SheetEdit.MoveSheet(0));</code></pre>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>The Sheet operations are edits like any other.</b> <code>SheetEdit.InsertSheet</code>,
                <code>Rename</code>, <code>MoveSheet</code>, <code>CopySheet</code> and <code>DeleteSheet</code> act on the
                Sheet you name, each one step on the undo stack. The Sheet shown stays shown, unless it is the one deleted;
                then the Sheet that takes its place is. A name the Workbook does not have throws
                <code>ArgumentException</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b><code>SheetsChanged</code> hears every change to the Sheets</b> — yours, the user's, and an undo or redo
                of either — with the names in order before and after, and the Sheet shown. It is raised after
                <code>WorkbookChanged</code>. Showing another Sheet changes no Sheet, and is not raised.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>A command that acts on the Selection acts on the Sheet shown</b>, where the Selection is:
                <code>SetCellFormatAsync</code>, <code>SetNumberFormatAsync</code>, <code>SetAlignmentAsync</code> and
                <code>SetCellFormatFromFocusAsync</code> read the Selection and its Sheet together, when they run, as the
                toolbar's buttons do.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Sheet Tabs">
        <p>
            The strip under the grid holds a tab for each Sheet, in order, the shown Sheet's marked. Pressing a tab
            shows its Sheet and leaves the keyboard in the grid; so do <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Page Up</kbd> and
            <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Page Down</kbd>, which show the Sheet before or after, as in Excel, and stop at
            the first and the last. <kbd>Tab</kbd> from the grid reaches the strip, where <kbd>←</kbd>,
            <kbd>→</kbd>, <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd> select a tab and show its Sheet, and <kbd>Enter</kbd> or
            <kbd>Escape</kbd> hands the keyboard back to the grid. A press on a tab and a key on the strip take their
            place among the Sheet's keys: what is typed straight after them, over Blazor Server too, lands on the Sheet
            they show, at its own Focus, or in the grid they hand the keyboard back to.
        </p>
        <p>
            The tabs also add, rename, move, copy and delete Sheets, as Excel's do, and every one of them works from
            the keyboard alone:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>Add.</b> <code>+</code> after the tabs adds an empty Sheet after the one shown;
                <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>F11</kbd>, in the grid or on a tab, adds one before it, as Excel's does. It is
                named <code>Sheet</code> and the next number no Sheet has, and shown.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Rename.</b> Double-click a tab, or choose Rename on its menu, and the name is edited on the tab, all of
                it selected. <kbd>Enter</kbd> commits it, <kbd>Escape</kbd> keeps the old one, and pressing elsewhere
                — another tab, <code>+</code> — commits it, as in Excel. Every Formula that names the Sheet is
                rewritten. Begun from the keyboard on a tab, <kbd>Enter</kbd> and <kbd>Escape</kbd> give the keyboard
                back to the tab; begun with the pointer, to the grid. While an IME composes, its <kbd>Enter</kbd> and
                <kbd>Escape</kbd> are its own. A name Excel refuses (empty,
                longer than 31 characters, holding one of <code>: \ / ? * [ ]</code>, beginning or ending with an
                apostrophe, <code>History</code>, or another Sheet's name in any case) is refused, the notice says which
                rule it broke, and the name stays open to correct.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>The tab's menu.</b> Right-click a tab, or press <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>F10</kbd> or the menu key on
                it, for Rename, Delete, Move Left, Move Right and Copy. <kbd>&uarr;</kbd> and <kbd>&darr;</kbd> move among
                the commands that can be done, <kbd>Enter</kbd> does one, <kbd>Escape</kbd> closes the menu. Delete cannot
                be done on the Workbook's only Sheet, nor Move Left on the first or Move Right on the last, and each says
                why. Copy places <code>Name (2)</code> after the Sheet, with its Entries, formats and widths, and shows it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Drag.</b> Press a tab and release it on another: it takes that tab's place, and its Sheet is shown.
                Released past the last tab, on <code>+</code> or the strip's empty end, it goes last; released anywhere
                else in the strip, or taken off it, the drag ends with nothing moved.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Each is one step, and is said.</b> <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Z</kbd> undoes it; a deleted Sheet comes back
                with every Formula that read it, since ExSheet asks nothing before deleting. The undo shows the Sheet it
                brings back, or the one it leaves: a Sheet deleted, renamed or moved is shown, and when a Sheet added or
                copied is taken away, the Sheet it was added beside or copied from is shown. A redo shows the Sheet added
                or copied again; a Sheet deleted again is taken away, and the Sheet shown stays shown, unless it is the
                one deleted, when the Sheet that takes its place is. Either way the Sheet keeps its
                own Selection: a Sheet operation changes no cells to select. The Sheet's notice, its live
                region, says what was done, and <code>SheetsChanged</code> is raised. While a cell is being edited none of
                them is done, and the notice says why.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Each takes its place among the keys.</b> A key typed straight after <code>+</code>, a menu command, a
                drag or the <kbd>Enter</kbd> that commits a name, over Blazor Server too, acts on what it left: the Sheet
                added or shown, back in the grid.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Drawn by the Chrome.</b> With <code>MudSheetChrome</code> the tabs' names, the <code>+</code> button and
                the menu are drawn in MudBlazor's controls; what each press does is ExSheet's under either Chrome. A Chrome
                of your own draws them through <code>ISheetChrome.SheetTab</code>, <code>SheetTabAdd</code> and
                <code>SheetTabMenu</code>.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <b>Each Sheet keeps its own view.</b> The Selection and the scroll position a Sheet had when you left it
                are back when you return; a Sheet shown for the first time starts at A1 with nothing selected. Column
                widths are recorded in each Sheet.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>A gesture is taken against the Sheet it was aimed at.</b> A paste, a <kbd>Delete</kbd> or typing that
                reaches the component after another Sheet was shown is refused, as one aimed at a replaced document is,
                and the Sheet's notice says so; it is never applied to the Sheet now shown. On Blazor Server the two
                can cross on the wire.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>The Name Box goes to another Sheet.</b> Type the Sheet's name before the address, as a Reference
                names it — <code>Summary!B5</code>, <code>'Q1 Sales'!A1:C3</code> — and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>: that
                Sheet is shown with the cells selected and scrolled into view, in place of the view it kept. A name the
                Workbook does not hold moves nothing, and the notice says the address could not be read.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>A Formula points at another Sheet's cells.</b> While a Formula is typed, press a tab, or
                <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Page Down</kbd>: that Sheet is shown and the edit stays open, its text in the
                Formula Bar. An arrow or a press there writes the Reference with the Sheet's name,
                <code>=Summary!B1</code>, from that Sheet's own Selection, and the next point replaces it.
                <kbd>Enter</kbd> commits the Formula on the Sheet it was typed on, shows that Sheet again and moves
                down there; <kbd>Escape</kbd> cancels it and shows that Sheet again too.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>While a constant is typed the Sheet shown stays.</b> A tab pressed then, or another Sheet's address
                typed into the Name Box while any edit is open, is refused, and the Sheet's notice says to finish the
                edit first.
            </li>
            <li>
                The strip is as tall as the Formula Bar, and the height you give ExSheet includes it.
                <code>ShowSheetTabs="false"</code> hides it; the Sheets stay, and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> +
                <kbd>Page Up</kbd> and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>Page Down</kbd> still show them.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

